## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit data as four independent 32-bit lanes and perform lane-wise rotation without loops. Demonstrates precise bit-slicing, correct boundary handling, and minimal, provable code paths.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit packing into 4×32 lanes
- Lane-wise rotation using (x<<n) | (x>>(32-n))
- Bit masking to confine intermediate results to 32-bit lanes
- Clamping n with & 31 to handle n >= 32

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64 lo; uint64 hi; } uint128;

uint128 rotateWithinLanes128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
    n &= 31;
    uint32 a = (uint32)(x.lo);
    uint32 b = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
    uint32 c = (uint32)(x.hi);
    uint32 d = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);

    x.lo = ((uint64)((a << n) | (a >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((b << n) | (b >> (32 - n)));
    x.hi = ((uint64)((c << n) | (c >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((d << n) | (d >> (32 - n)));
    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of cross-limb bit indexing and non-trivial bit-twiddling without branching. It introduces a different angle: packing every other bit across a 128-bit value into a 64-bit result.

## Key Concepts

- **Piece-wise bit extraction with masks:** Isolate target bits using bitwise AND with appropriate masks.
- **In-limb and cross-limb mapping:** Map even bits within each 64-bit limb, then combine results.
- **Parallel bit-pack technique:** Use the classic "compress" trick—mask, shift, and merge repeatedly to collapse bits.
- **Endianness and limb numbering:** The `lo` limb contains bits 0-63, and `hi` contains bits 64-127 (little-endian representation).

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint64_t packEvenBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;              // keep even bits
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  lo = (lo | (lo >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi &= 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 1)) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 2)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 4)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 8)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  hi = (hi | (hi >> 16)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL;

  return (hi << 32) | lo;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-word bit ops and branchless parity reduction. It exercises combining two 64-bit halves and a compact reduction, scalable to wider architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Parity via XOR folding
- 128-bit composition hi^lo
- Branchless reductions

## Code Example

```c
int parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return x & 1;
}
```

